            <ArticleTitle>[Comorbidities in psoriasic arthritis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>734-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Psoriatic arthritis occurs almost exclusively together with psoriasis of the skin. It seems useful therefore for the practitioner to consider the two of them as manifestations of one &quot;psoriatic disease&quot;. This pragmatic approach is more contemporary, particularly since it is increasingly clear that the &quot;psoriatic disease&quot; goes beyond damage to the skin and joints. The most important &quot;comorbidities&quot; associated with psoriatic disease are presented here; especially the cardiovascular diseases as well as their risk factors, namely smoking, obesity, and the metabolic syndrome. Moreover, infections, osteoporosis and neoplasia are to be considered.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
